    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d527">

                <pb facs="0622" n="610" sameAs="frus:frus1940v03/pg610"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.6831/241: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-01-20T13:00:00+02:00">January 20, 1940—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 20—10:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>11. Complying with instruction No. 622 of September 28, 1939,<anchor n="94"
                        xml:id="d527-f1"/> I laid before the Under Secretary for Foreign Affairs and
                    the Ministry of National Economy this morning the Department’s views and
                    proposals regarding the negotiation of a possible trade agreement. Further talks
                    which may be necessary to facilitate the Greek Government reaching a decision
                    regarding its readiness to negotiate can be carried on directly with the
                    Minister of National Economy. While of necessity noncommittal at this stage,
                    both Ministers appeared receptive to our advances and the latter, especially,
                    expressed a hopeful attitude.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
